**Proposition 3.2 (original finite exposure under its interface hypothesis).**
Let $d$ be the native segment length through the read in Theorem 3.1.
Suppose that, at every paired cut of this segment, the actual unchanged
original interface admits Advance as precisely the prescribed next
native row, with its actual guard, label, releases and complete original
successor, including the inherited latch4. This original-interface
hypothesis is not supplied by native enabledness and remains unverified
for the annex segment. Under this hypothesis,

$$
x_0\not\sim_Q x_1.
$$

On $\{x_0,x_1\}$, each of GENERAL's four views separately has exactly
the equality kernel of $Q$.

Proof. The fixed nonadaptive experiment consisting of $d$ Advances
belongs to the original language under the stated hypothesis. It uses
no test of private coordinates. Each of its rows really executes; the
last complete original successors have different latch4 values by
Theorem 3.1, so its responses differ. No intermediate response, label
or receipt is discarded. Equal histories give equal partial response
maps, and Theorem 2.2 gives the two distinct values of each view. These
facts prove the restricted kernel assertion. ∎

The finite native exposure is unconditional within Assumption 1.2's
model. Its interpretation as an original experiment has exactly the
additional hypothesis in Proposition 3.2. Failure to establish that
hypothesis does not establish equality of the two complete $Q$ maps.

